<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donnchad Ua Cerbaill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1168">1168</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Donough O'Carroll</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donnchad Ua Cerbaill was king of Airgialla in the north of Ireland in the 12th century. He extended his realm into Louth and was a major supporter of church reform, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a> in 1142 with St Malachy, the first Cistercian monastery in Ireland. He was killed in 1168.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Donnchad was a half-brother on his mother's side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a>, King of Bréifne: both were sons of Aillend, daughter of Ua Baegelláin, a local ruler of the Fir Manach. His family's base was the Clann Cearbhaill lands in Fearmaigh and west Louth. He rose in the age historians call that of the High Kings with Opposition, roughly 1076 to 1166, when provincial armies repeatedly crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">County Louth</a>, the Kingdom of Midhe was divided again and again, and outside candidates were often imposed as local kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his career Donnchad conquered the sub-kingdom of Conaille Muirtheimne in north Louth, around 1130, raided across Meath into Fingal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlement north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, and worked closely with the northern king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-mac-lochlainn">Muirchertach Mac Lochlainn</a>. By about 1140 he had added Fir Arda Ciannachta and extended his influence around Newry and Bairrche in south County Down. He settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> groups of Airgialla on his conquests: the Uí Méith gave their name to Omeath and the Mugdorna to the Mournes.</p><p>His church foundations served his power as well as reform. Besides Mellifont he was connected with foundations at Louth, Knock Abbey and Termonfeckin, some of them of the Arrouaisian order. Handing older religious sites from local dynasts to European orders weakened them as bases of opposition. To reflect his conquests, the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">County Louth</a> passed from the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> to that of Clogher, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> styled himself Bishop of Louth. In 1166, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-mac-lochlainn">Muirchertach Mac Lochlainn</a> broke an oath and blinded Donnchad's foster-son Eochaid Mac Duinn Sléibe, king of Ulaid, Donnchad was central to the overthrow and killing of the High King.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a>, Donnchad was mortally wounded while drunk by the battle-axe of one of his own servants, Ua Duibhne of the Cenél nEoghain. He was buried in the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a>, in an arched recess on the north side of the high altar. Before his death he had placed Áed in Macáem Tóinlesc on the throne of Ailech, dividing that kingdom's power for 75 years. By 1193 his family had been eclipsed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">County Louth</a> had been lost to the Norman colony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donnchad Ua Cerbaill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 12th century <b>Donnchad Ua Cerbaill</b>, king of Airgialla, extended his realm into Louth and founded Mellifont Abbey in 1142, the first Cistercian house in Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-airgialla">Kingdom of Airgialla</a></li></ul></section>
